    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d783">

                <pb facs="0868" n="582" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg582"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 300.115/6737</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-01-21">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 21, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received 9.45 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1230. Your 1298, 5th. Foreign Office replies that it is not possible to grant a
                    general authorization for the free exportation of hops as requested, adding that
                    hops of a similar quality can, as a matter of fact, be found in Russia and in
                    France and it does not appear that the brewery industry of the United States
                    depends on hops of German and Austrian origin.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sharp</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
